-- §3 head projection at a CONSTRAINED headed impl BESIDE A PLAIN SIBLING — the
-- #1630 row that grades the SILENT-WRONGNESS channel. Third pin of the fix,
-- after `s3-constrained-impl-head-routes.mdk` (one impl, `Eq a => Int`) and
-- `s3-constrained-effect-impl-head-routes.mdk` (stacked wrappers).
--
-- 🚨 THIS ROW EXISTS BECAUSE THE OTHER TWO CANNOT SEE THE WORST CELL. Both of
-- them are ONE-IMPL programs, and on a one-impl program the pre-fix `check` and
-- `run` were already CORRECT — the defect surfaced only at `build`, loudly. That
-- is a property of ONE IMPL, not of the defect. Put a second impl of the same
-- interface in the file and the pre-fix `run` is WRONG AT EXIT 0:
--
--   `impl Sz (Eq a => Box Int)` -> 13   and   `impl Sz (Box Bool)` -> 14
--
-- SPEC ANSWER, hand-derived from the two impl bodies before any binary was run:
-- `sz (Box 1)` selects the `Box Int` impl (13), `sz (Box True)` selects the
-- `Box Bool` impl (14), so `main` prints **27**. It is not a recapture.
--
-- MEASURED on a base arm built from this branch with the one-line fix REVERTED
-- (`headTyNode (TyConstrained _ t) = headTyNode t` deleted, full 2-stage
-- rebuild), against the fix arm, redirect-then-read of `$?` and `MEDAKA_STRICT=1`
-- on every cell:
--
--            | check | run           | build | executed binary
--   ---------|-------|---------------|-------|-----------------
--   BEFORE   |   0   | 0, prints 26  |   1   | — no binary
--   AFTER    |   0   | 0, prints 27  |   0   | 0, prints 27
--
-- AND IT IS DECLARATION-ORDER-DECIDED: reversing the two impl blocks makes the
-- pre-fix answer **28** (14 + 14), still exit 0, still `check` clean. The fix arm
-- prints 27 in BOTH orders — which is what Section 4's permutation pair asserts.
--
-- **26 = 13 + 13.** Both calls took the constrained impl. `eval.headTycon` peels
-- `TyConstrained` and filed `Eq a => Box Int` under tag `Box`, while
-- `typecheck.headTyNode` did not and stamped the caller side `__none__`, so the
-- constrained impl left the `Box` bucket on one side only and the two impls
-- stopped being discriminable. That is a WRONG VALUE AT EXIT 0 with `check`
-- clean — silent wrongness, which the one-impl rows grade nothing of.
--
-- ⚠️ SO READ #1630's S1 GRADING AS SCOPED TO ITS REPORTED SHAPE. The class it
-- belongs to reaches S0. The issue is not misgraded; the report simply did not
-- carry this program. Two more pre-fix `run` failures the same one-line peel
-- removes, measured on the same base arm and deliberately NOT given files of
-- their own (they ride the identical arm): a `requires`-bearing constrained head
-- (`impl Sz (Eq c => Box b) requires Eq b`) died `E-NOT-A-FUNCTION: applied
-- non-function: 21`, and a constrained head in RETURN position beside a plain
-- sibling died `E-NONEXHAUSTIVE-MATCH` — the wrong impl's value reached the
-- match. Both are green on the fix arm (21 and 107 on every cell).
-- ⚠️ These two error STRINGS are what THIS repair round measured. A brief that
-- routed this work quoted them as `applied non-function: 1` and
-- `E-PANIC: unknown op '+'`; the second was not reproduced here on any shape
-- tried, and a one-impl return-position constrained head is simply CORRECT on
-- the base arm (run 106, only `build` fails). Record what you ran.
--
-- ⚠️ TWO IMPL BLOCKS OF ONE INTERFACE, so Section 4 DOES derive a permutation
-- pair here — unlike its two siblings, and deliberately. The pre-fix answer was
-- decided by which impl was declared first, exactly as in #1617, so the
-- permutation is the half a single-order value pin passes by construction.
--
-- ⚠️ THE TWO HEADS SHARE A HEAD TYCON (`Box`) ON PURPOSE. Post-fix both impls
-- project to `Box`, so this row also asserts that peeling the wrapper does not
-- collapse `Box Int` and `Box Bool` into one another — the full head type still
-- discriminates below the tag.
interface Sz a where
  sz : a -> Int

data Box a = Box a

impl Sz (Eq a => Box Int) where
  sz _ = 13

impl Sz (Box Bool) where
  sz _ = 14

main = println (sz (Box 1) + sz (Box True))
